{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1778783201,"nonce":"Sq96gWMjHXbTs3WDW0ZsYA87RQw=","title":"Use Equinox `__check_init__` instead of `__post_init__` on Graph so validation runs in subclasses","message":"When the web backend boots, Equinox prints this UserWarning:\n\n\u003e Class `feedbax.bodies.SimpleFeedback` has both an `__init__` method and a `__post_init__` method. This means that the `__post_init__` method will not be run!\n\nThe `__post_init__` is inherited from `Graph` (feedbax/graph.py:191). It calls `_validate_graph()`, which enforces graph topology invariants:\n- every entry in `self.nodes` is a `Component`\n- every wire's `source_node` / `target_node` references an existing node\n- every input/output binding references a port that is declared on its node\n\n`SimpleFeedback` (bodies.py:140) defines its own `__init__` (bodies.py:150) that calls `super().__init__(...)` (bodies.py:257). Per Python dataclass semantics, a user-defined `__init__` does NOT automatically call `__post_init__`, so **`_validate_graph` is silently skipped every time `SimpleFeedback` is constructed**. Wiring bugs that should raise at construction would only surface later, at execution.\n\nFix:\nRename `Graph.__post_init__` -\u003e `__check_init__`. `__check_init__` is an Equinox-specific extension that always runs after `__init__`, regardless of whether subclasses override `__init__` (https://docs.kidger.site/equinox/api/module/advanced_fields/#checking-invariants). The body of the method stays identical.\n\nAudit:\n- `_validate_graph` only reads `self.*`, no assignments - safe to run from `__check_init__` (which forbids assignment).\n- `Graph` is the only class in feedbax/ whose subclass overrides `__init__`. `SimpleFeedback` is the only direct `Graph` subclass; the other matches in grep (`GraphState`, `GraphMetadata`, `GraphSpec`, etc.) are Pydantic models or unrelated dataclasses.\n- No code explicitly calls `__post_init__()` anywhere (only comment references in `loss.py:1205` and `analysis/plot.py:94`).\n\nTest:\nAfter the fix, construct a `SimpleFeedback` with a deliberately invalid wire (e.g., wire pointing at a node name not in `self.nodes`) and confirm it raises `ValueError` at instantiation. The current behaviour silently accepts it.","files":null}]}